          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How do I know whether a three-character category can stand on its own as a usable code?",
          "answer": "In ICD-10-CM, a three-character code represents a category. This category itself may sometimes be usable on its own, while in other cases, further characters are needed for specificity. For example, the three-character code I10 indicates hypertension and can be used alone. In contrast, the asthma category J45 is never used at three characters and requires additional detail to be applicable.\n\nTo determine if a three-character category is usable on its own, you can check the specific guidelines related to that category, including whether it includes necessary details such as severity or complications. You can refer to the ICD-10-CM code structure document for this information, which explains that three characters form the category, and characters four and five provide more detail.\n\nIf you have other questions or need more specific guidance, feel free to ask!\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
